    <string name="contacts">Contacts in search</string>
    <string name="contacts_desc">Type a name to call, text or open a WhatsApp chat. Search also answers sums (12*7), finds settings (wifi, brilho) and the flashlight</string>
    <string name="grant_contacts">Tap to allow contacts</string>
    <string name="standby_charging">Standby while charging</string>
    <string name="standby_charging_desc">A dim bedside clock after 20 seconds on the home screen while charging. Pinch the home screen with two fingers to open it any time; tap to leave. It stays lit all night only over the lock screen, so nobody can reach your notifications: with Minimalist on in Accessibility, it locks the phone itself after 10 untouched seconds and lights straight up again, and it is back on top every time the screen turns off on the charger. Without it, a still-unlocked phone times out like any screen</string>
    <string name="standby_wake">Standby on every wake</string>
    <string name="standby_wake_desc">Each time the screen turns on, the bedside clock shows first, over the lock screen. Tap it to unlock, or unlock with your fingerprint or face; you return to the app you were in. The same switch lets home, recents or pulling down the notifications lock the phone on a still-unlocked bedside clock</string>
    <string name="grant_accessibility">Tap to turn on Minimalist in Accessibility</string>
    <string name="accessibility_title">Before turning on Accessibility</string>
    <string name="accessibility_disclosure">Minimalist uses Android\'s accessibility service only for two things an ordinary app isn\'t allowed to do: lock the screen (a double-tap on the home screen, or leaving the bedside clock while the phone is still unlocked) and show the bedside clock when the screen turns on.\n\nIt doesn\'t read what is on your screen, sees nothing you type, watches no other app and sends nothing off your phone. You can turn it off at any time in Android\'s accessibility settings.</string>
